    "authorAssociation": "NONE",
    "body": "### Contributing guidelines\r\n\r\n- [X] I have read [CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md](https://github.com/nvim-mini/mini.nvim/blob/main/CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md)\r\n\r\n### Module(s)\r\n\r\nmini.pick\r\n\r\n### Question\r\n\r\nFirst of all, I want to say that the job done on these plugins are awesome, congrats to the developer.\r\n\r\nThat said, I'm facing a small visual annoyance where the `<Tab>` character is being rendered as `>` inside the mini.pick preview window. Curiously, this does not happen in mini.files preview (and in any other buffer), which looks clean out-of-the-box.\r\n\r\n| mini.files preview (desired) | mini.pick preview (undesired) |\r\n|------------------------------|-------------------------------|\r\n| <img width=\"872\" height=\"568\" alt=\"image\" src=\"https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/6e6151d4-512b-44c7-be49-8195b43b8d10\" /> | <img width=\"874\" height=\"678\" alt=\"image\" src=\"https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/2282cf9d-e589-4e33-9afe-738658cbb84e\" /> |",
    "bodyHTML": "<h3 dir=\"auto\">Contributing guidelines</h3>\n<ul class=\"contains-task-list\">\n<li class=\"task-list-item\"><input type=\"checkbox\" id=\"\" disabled=\"\" class=\"task-list-item-checkbox\" aria-label=\"Completed task\" checked=\"\"> I have read <a href=\"https://github.com/nvim-mini/mini.nvim/blob/main/CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md\">CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md</a></li>\n</ul>\n<h3 dir=\"auto\">Module(s)</h3>\n<p dir=\"auto\">mini.pick</p>\n<h3 dir=\"auto\">Question</h3>\n<p dir=\"auto\">First of all, I want to say that the job done on these plugins are awesome, congrats to the developer.</p>\n<p dir=\"auto\">That said, I'm facing a small visual annoyance where the <code class=\"notranslate\">&lt;Tab&gt;</code> character is being rendered as <code class=\"notranslate\">&gt;</code> inside the mini.pick preview window. Curiously, this does not happen in mini.files preview (and in any other buffer), which looks clean out-of-the-box.</p>\n<markdown-accessiblity-table><table role=\"table\">\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>mini.files preview (desired)</th>\n<th>mini.pick preview (undesired)</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://private-user-images.githubusercontent.com/35900428/535978097-6e6151d4-512b-44c7-be49-8195b43b8d10.png?jwt=eyJ0eXAiOiJKV1QiLCJhbGciOiJIUzI1NiJ9..JrKiHjxJXIlVGT_2SMuaq6O_oQ1iUfuURH49e7Ps9Bo\"><img width=\"872\" height=\"568\" alt=\"image\" src=\"https://private-user-images.githubusercontent.com/35900428/535978097-6e6151d4-512b-44c7-be49-8195b43b8d10.png?jwt=eyJ0eXAiOiJKV1QiLCJhbGciOiJIUzI1NiJ9..JrKiHjxJXIlVGT_2SMuaq6O_oQ1iUfuURH49e7Ps9Bo\" style=\"max-width: 100%; height: auto; max-height: 568px;; aspect-ratio: 872 / 568; background-color: var(--bgColor-muted); border-radius: 6px\" class=\"js-gh-image-fallback\"></a></td>\n<td><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://private-user-images.githubusercontent.com/35900428/535978520-2282cf9d-e589-4e33-9afe-738658cbb84e.png?jwt=eyJ0eXAiOiJKV1QiLCJhbGciOiJIUzI1NiJ9..vqYZ7_EPGgBefsdAxE_uP6EdQFVEr40F8-gh3Yk_n8M\"><img width=\"874\" height=\"678\" alt=\"image\" src=\"https://private-user-images.githubusercontent.com/35900428/535978520-2282cf9d-e589-4e33-9afe-738658cbb84e.png?jwt=eyJ0eXAiOiJKV1QiLCJhbGciOiJIUzI1NiJ9..vqYZ7_EPGgBefsdAxE_uP6EdQFVEr40F8-gh3Yk_n8M\" style=\"max-width: 100%; height: auto; max-height: 678px;; aspect-ratio: 874 / 678; background-color: var(--bgColor-muted); border-radius: 6px\" class=\"js-gh-image-fallback\"></a></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></markdown-accessiblity-table>",
    "bodyText": "Contributing guidelines\n\n I have read CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md\n\nModule(s)\nmini.pick\nQuestion\nFirst of all, I want to say that the job done on these plugins are awesome, congrats to the developer.\nThat said, I'm facing a small visual annoyance where the <Tab> character is being rendered as > inside the mini.pick preview window. Curiously, this does not happen in mini.files preview (and in any other buffer), which looks clean out-of-the-box.\n\n\n\nmini.files preview (desired)\nmini.pick preview (undesired)",
    "category": {
        "createdAt": "2022-01-02T14:24:09Z",
        "description": "Ask the community for help",
        "emoji": ":pray:",
        "emojiHTML": "<div>🙏</div>",
        "id": "DIC_kwDOFoksYM4CAgqN",
        "isAnswerable": true,
        "name": "Q&A",
        "slug": "q-a",
        "updatedAt": "2022-01-02T14:24:09Z"
    },
    "closed": true,
    "closedAt": "2026-01-16T14:39:46Z",
    "comment_count": 2,
    "comment_data": [
        {
            "author": {
                "avatarUrl": "https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/24854248?u=d09fd974c6720eba3039db6beb2f1c1efecdb51d&v=4",
                "login": "echasnovski",
                "resourcePath": "/echasnovski",
                "url": "https://github.com/echasnovski"
            },
            "authorAssociation": "MEMBER",
            "body": "> First of all, I want to say that the job done on these plugins are awesome, congrats to the developer.\r\n\r\n:heart:\r\n\r\n> That said, I'm facing a small visual annoyance where the `<Tab>` character is being rendered as `>` inside the mini.pick preview window. Curiously, this does not happen in mini.files preview (and in any other buffer), which looks clean out-of-the-box.\r\n\r\nIt probably comes from 'listchars' option. Could you check the (global) value of 'list' and 'listchars' options? Type `:=vim.o.list, vim.o.listchars` and see what it prints. I think 'listchars' should contain an entry like `tab:> `, but (judging by the existence of this issue) the  'list' is probably `false`.\r\n\r\nThere is indeed discrepancy in how 'mini.pick' and 'mini.files' handle 'listchars'. This comes from the fact that main 'mini.pick' window is also meant to show `…` if there is extra text to the right, which it does via `extends:…` entry in 'listchars'.\r\n\r\nI'll take a look at how to best approach this.",
            "bodyHTML": "<blockquote>\n<p dir=\"auto\">First of all, I want to say that the job done on these plugins are awesome, congrats to the developer.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p dir=\"auto\">❤️</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p dir=\"auto\">That said, I'm facing a small visual annoyance where the <code class=\"notranslate\">&lt;Tab&gt;</code> character is being rendered as <code class=\"notranslate\">&gt;</code> inside the mini.pick preview window. Curiously, this does not happen in mini.files preview (and in any other buffer), which looks clean out-of-the-box.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p dir=\"auto\">It probably comes from 'listchars' option. Could you check the (global) value of 'list' and 'listchars' options? Type <code class=\"notranslate\">:=vim.o.list, vim.o.listchars</code> and see what it prints. I think 'listchars' should contain an entry like <code class=\"notranslate\">tab:&gt; </code>, but (judging by the existence of this issue) the  'list' is probably <code class=\"notranslate\">false</code>.</p>\n<p dir=\"auto\">There is indeed discrepancy in how 'mini.pick' and 'mini.files' handle 'listchars'. This comes from the fact that main 'mini.pick' window is also meant to show <code class=\"notranslate\">…</code> if there is extra text to the right, which it does via <code class=\"notranslate\">extends:…</code> entry in 'listchars'.</p>\n<p dir=\"auto\">I'll take a look at how to best approach this.</p>",
            "bodyText": "First of all, I want to say that the job done on these plugins are awesome, congrats to the developer.\n\n❤️\n\nThat said, I'm facing a small visual annoyance where the <Tab> character is being rendered as > inside the mini.pick preview window. Curiously, this does not happen in mini.files preview (and in any other buffer), which looks clean out-of-the-box.\n\nIt probably comes from 'listchars' option. Could you check the (global) value of 'list' and 'listchars' options? Type :=vim.o.list, vim.o.listchars and see what it prints. I think 'listchars' should contain an entry like tab:> , but (judging by the existence of this issue) the  'list' is probably false.\nThere is indeed discrepancy in how 'mini.pick' and 'mini.files' handle 'listchars'. This comes from the fact that main 'mini.pick' window is also meant to show … if there is extra text to the right, which it does via extends:… entry in 'listchars'.\nI'll take a look at how to best approach this.",
